{% set homeowner_guide %}
<p>Quick reference for Paint Rock Valley property owners:</p>

<p><strong>Think about where water comes from.</strong> In a valley, your property collects more than just rain that falls on your roof. Walk your land after a storm — where does water flow onto your property from uphill? Understanding these patterns helps design effective drainage.</p>

<p><strong>Size up for volume.</strong> Standard 5-inch gutters may not be enough for valley properties that see heavy runoff. 6-inch gutters with 3x4 downspouts handle greater flow rates without backing up.</p>

<p><strong>Get water away from the foundation.</strong> On valley floors, water doesn't drain away naturally — the ground is relatively flat. Downspout extensions need to move water well away from foundations. Underground drains may be necessary for serious drainage problems.</p>

<p><strong>Watch for humidity damage.</strong> Living near the river means checking gutters and fascia for early signs of rot or corrosion. Catching problems early prevents expensive repairs later.</p>

<p><strong>Seasonal timing for <a href="/recent-projects/">Paint Rock</a> properties:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>March:</strong> Pre-storm season inspection — check for winter damage before spring rains</li>
<li><strong>June:</strong> Mid-summer check — storms may have deposited debris</li>
<li><strong>November:</strong> Post-leaf cleanup once hardwoods are bare</li>
</ul>

<p><strong>Signs your system needs attention:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Water overflowing gutters during moderate (not just heavy) rain</li>
<li>Erosion channels forming where water drains</li>
<li>Pooling water near foundation after storms</li>
<li>Visible rust, rot, or deterioration on gutters or fascia</li>
<li>Gutters pulling away from the house</li>
</ul>

